From 5fae380a73a3bb991ab47b079c92f7fb53d21419 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emdee52 <emdee7101@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 17:18:58 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] port(MB-5,MB-6): move selected volumes into one object; merge
 with difference

Batch of two features on one branch, one build (they share the selection gate and
the split_parts tool).

MB-5 - Split selected parts to an object: moves every selected volume of one
object (parts, negatives, modifiers, support and Precise Seam volumes) into ONE
new object named <source>_parts and leaves the rest in the source. The volumes
are re-parented with the new ModelObject::move_volume_to, so type, name,
per-volume settings, painting, text metadata and placement are the very same
volumes. The MB-4 per-part action stays and is renamed "Split selected parts to
objects"; both live in the right-click Modify submenu, and the selection gate
grew a generic selected_volumes_of_one_object() that MB-3/MB-4 also use.

MB-6 - Merge selected parts can subtract: merge_parts_into_one() now accepts
NEGATIVE_VOLUME next to MODEL_PART, unions the positives first and subtracts the
negatives in object space, replaces the lowest selected positive and consumes
every selected negative (inside or outside). Modifiers/support/Precise Seam
volumes are refused whole; a kernel refusal with negatives selected aborts and
changes nothing. The Object List gate mirrors the rule.

MCP: split_parts gained one_object; boolean_parts drives the difference path
unchanged.

Tests: tests/libslic3r/test_model.cpp - moving volumes keeps types, names,
settings and world placement and refuses an all-volume selection; a merge with
an inside and an outside cutter leaves one part of 7936 mm^3 and consumes both
cutters, and refuses negatives-only, single-positive and modifier selections.

libslic3r suite 1736/1736 (109 493 assertions). Runtime over MCP:
split_parts(0,[1,2],one_object) took a 3-part object to a 1-part source plus
one 2-part object, and boolean_parts(1,[0,2]) with a negative took 3 volumes to
2 (36 -> 92 triangles on the cut part), both slicing idle.
